Part 1. Facts survey

### 1.1. Facts given in the task
- Review metadata:
  - Title: Cotrimoxazole prophylaxis for opportunistic infections in children with HIV infection
  - Objective: To assess the effects of routinely administered cotrimoxazole on death and illness episodes in children 
with HIV infection, and in infants of HIV-infected mothers.
  - Background:
    - Most HIV-infected children are in low-income countries without access to antiretroviral drugs.
    - Prevention and early treatment of opportunistic infections are key.
    - Cotrimoxazole (trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) is inexpensive, broad-spectrum, effective against Pneumocystis 
jiroveci pneumonia (PCP), and generally safe.
    - HIV diagnosis in children is complicated by maternal antibodies; prophylaxis based on maternal status is a 
possible approach but challenging in low-resource settings and may increase drug resistance.
  - Selection criteria: Randomised or quasi-randomised trials comparing routine cotrimoxazole versus placebo or no 
treatment in:
    - Children <15 years with HIV infection; or
    - Children <18 months with HIV-infected mothers.

- Provided study list (study_index, title, abstract):
  - Study 1: CHAP trial, Zambia
    - Design: Double-blind randomized placebo-controlled trial in children aged 1–14 years with clinical features of HIV
infection.
    - Primary outcomes: Mortality and adverse events possibly related to treatment.
    - Sample: 541 randomized; 7 later identified as HIV-negative and excluded (net analyzed N=534). Arm sizes not stated
in the abstract.
    - Follow-up: Median 19 months; trial stopped early on DSMC advice (October 2003).
    - Mortality: 74 (28%) in cotrimoxazole vs 112 (42%) in placebo; HR 0.57 (95% CI 0.43–0.77), p=0.0002.
    - Benefit consistent beyond 12 months (HR 0.48 [0.27–0.84]) and across ages and baseline CD4 counts (tests for 
heterogeneity non-significant).
    - Adverse events (grade 3/4): 16 (6%) cotrimoxazole vs 18 (7%) placebo; includes rash (one in placebo) and 
neutrophils <0.5×10^9/L (16 [6%] cotrimoxazole vs 7 [3%] placebo; p=0.06).
    - PCP identification: Found in 1 (placebo) of 73 nasopharyngeal aspirates in children with pneumonia.
    - Interpretation: Recommends prophylaxis for children with clinical features of HIV infection in resource-poor 
settings, irrespective of local resistance.
  - Study 2: Early chemoprophylaxis in adults (Abidjan, Côte d'Ivoire)
    - Population: HIV-infected adults (WHO stage 2 or 3).
    - Outcome: Severe clinical events reduced with cotrimoxazole; survival did not differ.
    - Not pediatric.
  - Study 3: TMP-SMX chemoprophylaxis for PCP in AIDS
    - Population: 60 patients with new Kaposi’s sarcoma; age not stated; likely adult context.
    - Design: Random assignment to TMP-SMX twice daily vs no therapy.
    - Outcomes: PCP prevention (0/30 vs 16/30), survival benefit; 50% adverse reactions.
    - Population not specified as pediatric.
  - Study 4: Case series in homosexual men (early AIDS description)
    - Not randomized; adult male population; not pediatric.
  - Study 5: Zimbabwe pediatric deaths (≤5 years) with necropsy
    - Observational; shows opportunistic infections and malnutrition associations; not randomized prophylaxis trial.
  - Study 6: Rwanda prospective cohort of infants born to HIV+ vs HIV− mothers
    - Observational natural history; not randomized prophylaxis trial.
  - Study 7: Malawi morbidity study in HIV-infected/uninfected children
    - Prospective observational; not randomized prophylaxis trial.
  - Study 8: TMP-SMX prophylaxis in HIV-infected adults with TB (Abidjan)
    - Adult TB population; randomized; not pediatric.
  - Study 9: Review on mother-to-child transmission (NEJM)
    - Not a randomized trial; background/review.
  - Study 10: South African HIV-infected children with pneumonia (PCP incidence)
    - Prospective diagnostic/observational; mentions prophylaxis association with reduced PCP; not randomized 
prophylaxis trial.

