Final answer: ### 1. Task outcome (short version):
Moderate-certainty evidence from one double-blind RCT in Zambia shows daily cotrimoxazole reduces mortality in 
HIV-infected children (HR 0.57; 95% CI 0.43–0.77), with similar serious adverse events and a non-significant trend 
toward more neutropenia. Effects were consistent across ages and CD4 strata and remained beyond 12 months, and were 
observed despite high local bacterial resistance—supporting applicability in low-resource/high-resistance settings. 
Evidence on illness episodes and on HIV-exposed uninfected infants is insufficient.

### 2. Task outcome (extremely detailed version):
Quality assessment
Study [1]: quality rating = moderate
- Strengths: Randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled design.; Intention-to-treat stated.; Consistent mortality 
benefit across ages and CD4 strata.; Objective primary outcome (all-cause mortality).; Conducted in a low-resource, 
high-resistance setting (Zambia).
- Limitations: Allocation concealment method not reported.; Early stopping for benefit may overestimate treatment 
effect.; Modified ITT suggested by excluding 7 subsequently HIV-negative participants.; Attrition and adherence details 
not reported.; Illness episodes not reported; limited safety detail.; Single-country setting limits external 
generalizability.
- Risk of bias:
  • Randomization: Randomized (method not described).
  • Allocation concealment: Allocation concealment not reported (unclear).
  • Blinding: Double-blind, placebo-controlled; outcome (mortality) objective -> low risk.
  • Attrition: Loss to follow-up not reported; seven later determined HIV-negative were excluded, suggesting modified 
ITT -> some concerns.
  • Early stopping: Trial stopped early (Oct 2003) for benefit -> risk of exaggerated effect.
  • Selective reporting: Illness episodes not reported; safety reported but limited granularity -> some concerns.

Narrative synthesis
Mortality: Daily cotrimoxazole prophylaxis significantly reduced all-cause mortality in HIV-infected children in Zambia 
[1]. The overall hazard ratio was 0.57 (95% CI 0.43–0.77; p=0.0002), with persistent benefit in those followed ≥12 
months (HR 0.48, 95% CI 0.27–0.84). Absolute mortality was 28% with cotrimoxazole vs 42% with placebo over a median of 
19 months.
Illness episodes: Not reported in the included study, representing an evidence gap.
Safety: Grade 3–4 adverse events were similar (6% vs 7%). Neutropenia showed a non-significant trend toward increase 
with cotrimoxazole (6% vs 3%; p=0.06). No excess rash was observed.
Consistency across subgroups: Benefits were consistent across ages (heterogeneity p=0.82) and baseline CD4 counts 
(p=0.36), and persisted beyond 12 months of follow-up (heterogeneity p=0.60) [1].
Applicability to low-resource/high-resistance settings: The trial was conducted in Zambia, a low-resource setting with 
high bacterial resistance to cotrimoxazole; mortality benefits were observed despite high resistance, supporting 
applicability [1].
Limitations and evidence gaps: Single RCT stopped early for benefit (possible effect inflation); allocation concealment 
and randomization methods not described; attrition and adherence not detailed; illness episodes not reported; no data on
HIV-exposed uninfected infants; long-term resistance and ART-era applicability not evaluated.

