Final answer: 1. Review Summary
- Title: Cotrimoxazole prophylaxis for opportunistic infections in children with HIV infection
- Objective: To assess the effects of routinely administered cotrimoxazole on death and illness episodes in children 
with HIV infection, and in infants of HIV‐infected mothers.

2. Search Results
- Number of candidate studies found: 10

3. Screening Results
- Included studies: 1
- Excluded studies: 9
- Decisions:
  • [1] Cotrimoxazole as prophylaxis against opportunistic infections in HIV‐infected Zambian children (CHAP): a 
double‐blind randomised placebo‐controlled trial: INCLUDE — Pediatric double-blind RCT; cotrimoxazole vs placebo.
  • [2] Early chemoprophylaxis with trimethoprim‐sulphamethoxazole for HIV‐1 infected adults in Abidjan, Cote d'Ivoire: 
a randomised trial: EXCLUDE — Adults; not pediatric.
  • [3] Safety and Efficacy of sulfamethoxazole and trimethoprim chemoprophylaxis for Pneumocystis carinii pneumonia in 
AIDS: EXCLUDE — Adults; not pediatric prophylaxis trial.
  • [4] Pneumocystis carinii pneumonia and mucosal candidiasis in previously healthy homosexual men: EXCLUDE — Not 
randomized; wrong population (adult case series).
  • [5] Pulmonary manifestations in HIV seropositivity and malnutrition in Zimbabwe: EXCLUDE — Observational; no 
randomized prophylaxis.
  • [6] Natural history of human immunodeficiency virus type 1 infection in children: Rwanda cohort: EXCLUDE — 
Observational; no randomized prophylaxis.
  • [7] Morbidity among HIV‐1 infected and uninfected African children: EXCLUDE — Observational; no randomized 
prophylaxis.
  • [8] TMP‐SMX prophylaxis in HIV‐infected adults with TB (Abidjan): RCT: EXCLUDE — Adults; not pediatric.
  • [9] Mother‐to‐child transmission of HIV (review): EXCLUDE — Narrative/epidemiologic review; not RCT.
  • [10] PCP in South African HIV‐infected children (prospective study): EXCLUDE — Observational/diagnostic; not 
randomized prophylaxis.

4. Evidence Extraction Summary (key PICO elements per included study)
- Study [1]
  Population: Children aged 1–14 years with clinical features of HIV infection in Zambia.
  Intervention: Daily co-trimoxazole (trimethoprim–sulfamethoxazole) prophylaxis.
  Comparator: Placebo.
  Follow-up: Median 19 months; subgroup analysis ≥12 months reported.
  Outcomes:
    • All-cause mortality (overall): HR 0.57 (95% CI 0.43–0.77); p=0.0002
    • All-cause mortality (≥12 months subgroup): HR 0.48 (95% CI 0.27–0.84)
  Adverse events:
    • Grade 3–4 adverse events (overall): 16 (6%) vs 18 (7%)
    • Neutrophil count <0.5×10^9/L (on one occasion): 16 (6%) vs 7 (3%); p=0.06
    • Rash: 0 vs 1 case
  Notes: Double-blind randomized placebo-controlled trial (intention-to-treat). Stopped early (Oct 2003) for benefit. 
Randomized n=541; 7 later identified as HIV-negative and excluded. Benefit consistent across ages and baseline CD4; 
effect present beyond 12 months. Very low PCP detection in NPAs despite high local bacterial resistance.
- Approximate absolute effect on mortality: 28% vs 42% (ARR 14.0 percentage points); NNT ≈ 7.1 (~7) over ~19 months.

5. Synthesis & Conclusions (Narrative synthesis)
- Quality evidence from one pediatric randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial (CHAP, Zambia) shows that daily
cotrimoxazole prophylaxis substantially reduces all-cause mortality among HIV-infected children aged 1–14 years compared
with placebo (HR 0.57, 95% CI 0.43–0.77).
- The mortality benefit persisted among those followed ≥12 months (HR 0.48, 95% CI 0.27–0.84) and did not vary 
meaningfully by age or baseline CD4 count, suggesting broad applicability across pediatric subgroups with clinical 
features of HIV.
- Serious adverse events were similar in cotrimoxazole and placebo groups (6% vs 7%), with a nonsignificant trend to 
more transient neutropenia in the cotrimoxazole arm (6% vs 3%, p=0.06) and very few rashes.
- Despite high local bacterial resistance to cotrimoxazole, mortality reduction was observed, indicating that 
prophylaxis confers benefit that is not negated by resistance patterns in this setting.
- Based on observed risks (28% vs 42%), the absolute risk reduction is about 14 percentage points, corresponding to a 
number needed to treat of approximately 7 over roughly 19 months.
- Data on illness episodes beyond mortality (e.g., hospitalizations, specific opportunistic infections) were limited in 
the pediatric RCT abstract; however, observational pediatric data suggest lower PCP incidence among children on 
prophylaxis. These observational findings were not included in the primary evidence due to study design.
- No randomized evidence was identified for routine cotrimoxazole prophylaxis in HIV-exposed but uninfected infants 
(based on maternal status), representing an important evidence gap.
- Implications: In resource-limited settings, cotrimoxazole prophylaxis should be considered routine for HIV-infected 
children, including in areas with high bacterial resistance. Programs should monitor hematologic tolerance and integrate
prophylaxis with broader HIV care and nutrition support.

6. Quality Assessment (evidence strength and limitations)
- Risk of bias: Low risk for blinding and randomization (double-blind randomized design; ITT analysis). Allocation 
concealment cannot be fully assessed from abstract but likely adequate given design; outcome ascertainment (mortality) 
is objective.
- Early stopping: The trial was stopped early for benefit, which can inflate effect estimates; this introduces some 
concern.
- Selective reporting: Mortality and adverse events were predefined primary outcomes; selective reporting risk appears 
low based on abstract.
- Attrition: Not explicitly detailed in abstract; intention-to-treat analysis was used, reducing attrition bias concern.
- Imprecision and inconsistency: The mortality effect is precise (95% CI does not cross 1); only one pediatric RCT is 
available, so between-study inconsistency cannot be assessed.
- Indirectness: Direct evidence for HIV-infected children 1–14 years. No RCT evidence for HIV-exposed uninfected 
infants; extrapolation to that group would be indirect.
- Publication bias: Not assessable with a single RCT.
- Overall certainty: Moderate to high for mortality reduction in HIV-infected children in similar low-resource settings;
downgraded slightly for early stopping and single-study evidence base.
